Drago’s counterpunch: light does not automatically eliminate shadow. When something blocks it, brighter light can cast a sharper shadow.

So Christ’s light is not merely overwhelming brightness; it is illumination that exposes the obstruction and asks us to surrender or transform it. The shadow isn’t an enemy-being to conquer—it is fear, deception, injury, pride, and unexamined appetite awaiting integration, repentance, or healing.

And “we will conquer all” needs discipline. Not all people. Not reality. Not every limit. We conquer what would make us cruel, dishonest, hopeless, or afraid to act—and even that is lifelong work.

The light wins not by destroying everything dark, but by making truth visible enough that darkness no longer governs our steps.